<title>USA: USDA to use dairy export subsidies aggressively.</title>
<headline>USDA to use dairy export subsidies aggressively.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-07-31</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Agriculture Secretary Dan Glickman Thursday said USDA would use the Dairy Export Incentive Program (DEIP) aggressively to target overseas markets.</p>
<p>&quot;International markets are critical to U.S. producers, particularly in light of slower growth in domestic demand over the past year and a half, unusually low farm-level milk prices and the continuing decline in the number of dairy operations in the U.S.,&quot; the secretary said in a statement.</p>
<p>He noted in the first month of the current DEIP year, which runs July 1997-June 1998, USDA awarded bonuses for the sale of over 18,000 tonnes of milk powder, butterfat and cheese, topping the total for the first six months of the prior year.</p>
<p>Glickman said based on market conditions, USDA anticipated exporting the full 137,446 tonnes of U.S. dairy products under DEIP this year allowed by international trade agreements.</p>
</text>
